    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">capital market</span> is the market for long-term funds — funds needed by governments and businesses for periods longer than one year. In Nigeria, the capital market has developed over several decades into one of the most important institutions in the economy, mobilising savings and channelling them into productive investment.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Origin and Development of the Nigerian Capital Market</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Before the establishment of a formal stock exchange, Nigerians who wished to buy or sell shares in companies (mostly foreign-owned) had to do so through brokers in London. This was slow, costly, and inconvenient. As the Nigerian economy grew after independence, the need for a local capital market became urgent.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">In <span class=\"learn-keyword\">1959</span>, the Federal Government set up the <strong>Barback Committee</strong> to advise on how to establish a stock exchange in Nigeria. This laid the groundwork for what became the Lagos Stock Exchange.</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Establishment of the Lagos Stock Exchange (1961)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Lagos Stock Exchange</span> was incorporated in <strong>1960</strong> and commenced trading in <strong>1961</strong> with only a handful of securities listed. It provided Nigerians, for the first time, with a local, organised market where shares, stocks, and bonds could be bought and sold under proper rules and supervision.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Transformation into the Nigerian Stock Exchange (NSE)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">In <strong>1977</strong>, the Lagos Stock Exchange was renamed the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Nigerian Stock Exchange (NSE)</span>, and branches (trading floors) were opened in other major cities such as Kaduna, Port Harcourt, Kano, Onitsha and Ibadan, to make the market accessible across the country. In <strong>2021</strong>, the NSE was further transformed into <strong>Nigerian Exchange Group (NGX Group)</strong>, with the Nigerian Exchange Limited (NGX) as its trading subsidiary.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Roles and Importance of the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Role of SEC</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Apex regulator</td><td>SEC is the apex regulatory body that oversees and regulates the entire Nigerian capital market.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Registration</td><td>Registers all securities, market operators (stockbrokers, issuing houses, registrars) before they can operate.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Investor protection</td><td>Protects investors from fraudulent and unfair market practices.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Approval of new issues</td><td>Approves new share issues, mergers, acquisitions and takeovers.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Market development</td><td>Develops rules and promotes public awareness of capital market activities.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Price stability</td><td>Maintains surveillance to prevent artificial pricing and market manipulation.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The SEC was established by the Securities and Exchange Commission Act, and it works above and supervises the operations of the Nigerian Exchange (NGX) itself.</p>\n",
