    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">stock exchange</span> is an organised market where existing stocks, shares, bonds and other long-term securities are bought and sold under established rules. It is different from the primary market, where brand-new securities are first issued to the public.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Functions of a Stock Exchange</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A stock exchange provides a regulated place and set of rules through which existing securities change hands. Its main functions include:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Provides liquidity</td><td>Allows investors to convert securities into cash quickly by selling on the exchange.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mobilises capital</td><td>Channels savings from individuals and institutions into companies and government for investment.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Determines prices</td><td>Prices of securities are determined by the forces of demand and supply (share valuation).</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Safety of transactions</td><td>Transactions are conducted under strict rules, protecting both buyers and sellers.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Economic barometer</td><td>The performance of the market (index) reflects the general health of the economy.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Members of the Stock Exchange</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Only registered <span class=\"learn-keyword\">stockbrokers</span> (dealing members) can buy and sell directly on the floor of the exchange on behalf of clients. Other key operators include issuing houses, registrars, trustees, and market makers, all licensed by the SEC.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Types of Securities Traded</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Security</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Ordinary shares</td><td>Give holders part-ownership of a company and a share of profit (dividend) that varies with performance.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Preference shares</td><td>Carry a fixed rate of dividend and are paid before ordinary shareholders.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Government bonds/stocks</td><td>Long-term loans to government that pay a fixed rate of interest.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Debentures/Corporate bonds</td><td>Long-term loans to a company, secured against its assets, paying fixed interest.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Importance to Investors, Companies and the Economy</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">To <strong>investors</strong>: safe place to invest and earn returns (dividends/capital gains). To <strong>companies</strong>: a source of long-term capital for expansion without heavy borrowing. To the <strong>economy</strong>: encourages savings, aids government financing, and attracts foreign investment.</div>\n",
