    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Having learnt what the SSM is and why it exists, we now look at how a company actually deals in SSM securities, and how the SSM compares with the main stock exchange.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Procedures for Dealing in SSM Securities</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>1. Appoint an issuing house</td><td>The company appoints an issuing house/sponsor to prepare and manage the share issue.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>2. Prepare a prospectus</td><td>A document giving details of the company and the offer is prepared and approved by the SEC.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>3. SEC approval</td><td>The Securities and Exchange Commission vets and approves the offer.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>4. Offer for subscription</td><td>Shares are offered to the investing public through the sponsor/stockbroker.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>5. Allotment and listing</td><td>Shares are allotted to successful applicants and the company is admitted to the SSM.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Advantages of the SSM</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">Lower listing costs; simpler requirements; access to capital without giving up excessive control; a chance to build public confidence gradually; and a pathway to eventually list on the main board.</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Challenges of the SSM</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Securities on the SSM tend to be less actively traded (lower liquidity) than main board securities; smaller companies may still find disclosure and reporting demands challenging; and investors sometimes perceive SSM-listed companies as higher-risk, which can affect investor confidence and share prices.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Comparison Between the Main Stock Exchange and the SSM</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Main/First-Tier Board</th><th>Second Tier (SSM)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Company size</td><td>Large, established companies</td><td>Small and medium-sized enterprises</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Listing requirements</td><td>Strict and demanding</td><td>Relaxed and easier to meet</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Minimum capital</td><td>High</td><td>Lower</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Trading activity</td><td>Usually higher (more liquid)</td><td>Usually lower</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n",
